Single-database configuration for Flask.

Schema history for the Correspondence Tracking System.

A note on what is in here, because it is not obvious from the filenames.

  97cf8ffed47d  baseline -- the schema as db.create_all() built it, before
                any hardening. Empty on purpose: it marks the starting point.
  0002_indexes  the ~30 indexes SDD 6.6.1 documents as present and which did
                not exist (D-04, Critical).
  0003_checks   the SDD 6.5.7 rules expressible as CHECK constraints.
  0004_refactions  the ON DELETE / ON UPDATE actions of R-12 / D-28.
  0005_triggers the automation and the rules a CHECK cannot express.

Revisions 0002 to 0005 were written against SQLite, which is what the
project ran on at the time. The system now runs on MySQL, where the schema
is created by schema/mysql_schema.sql -- generated from models.py and
carrying every index, constraint and trigger in one pass -- and the database
is then brought up to date with:

    python -m flask db stamp head

So on MySQL these four revisions are never executed. They are kept because
they are the record of how the schema was hardened and what each change was
for, and because Alembic needs an unbroken chain: the database records
0005_triggers in alembic_version, and any future revision is added on top of
it. Deleting them would strand that version and break every `flask db`
command.

New changes go in new revisions, generated against MySQL:

    python -m flask db migrate -m "what changed"   # then review the file
    python -m flask db upgrade
